    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Lithium-mediated nitrogen reduction (Li-N2R) is a promising electrochemical NH3 production alternative to the Haber Bosch process, a process responsible for 1.3% of CO2 emissions yearly.[1] While Haber Bosch utilizes high temperatures and pressures in large centralized facilities powered by fossil fuels, Li-N2R works at near-ambient temperatures and pressures allowing for decentralized production and can be powered by renewable electricity sources. In Li-N2R, Li ions are plated as Li metal on the working electrode, which then reacts with N2 gas to form lithium nitride (Li3N). Lithium nitride reacts with a proton source to form ammonia (NH3 ).\r\nThe solid electrolyte interphase (SEI) formed as a side product during Li-N2R has a large impact on the NH3 faradaic efficiency (FE) of the cell. Variations in the temperature during cell runs can influence both the thickness and composition of the SEI. This, in turn, alters the kinetics of species transport to and from the electrode. Consequently, SEIs formed at different temperatures result in different FEs, and are a promising tool to tune the reaction towards NH3 production.\r\nWe elucidate temperature effects on SEI formation and FE for electrolytes with 1M LiBF4 in Tetrahydrofuran (THF) with a 1v% EtOH proton source. By dissolving dried SEIs in a D2O rin- sate, we quantify the changing composition of the SEI formed at different temperatures using a combination of nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR), inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry (ICP-MS), and ion chromatography (IC). We also image the SEIs formed at different temperatures via Scanning Electron Microscopy (SEM) and analyze the electrochemical cycling data. By cor- relating SEI composition trends with changes in FE we provide pathways towards engineering the SEI for enhanced Li-N2R FE to NH3 . We find that above 22°C, the proportion of inorganics in the SEI dramatically increases, as does the thickness. This shift likely impedes species transport, as FE drops at loadings between 2.6 and 5.7 mg/cm2.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
